WORLD & EXPLORATION SYSTEM SPECIFICATION
Document: WORLD_AND_EXPLORATION_SPEC Version: 0.4 Status: Active Design Purpose: Defines the physical game world, regions, settlements, character travel paths, exploration, travel, safe areas, dangerous areas, Points of Interest, discovery and the relationship between exploration and the narrative.
 
⸻
 
1. DESIGN GOALS
The world should feel like a coherent place rather than a collection of disconnected RPG maps.
Each protagonist follows a linear main story along a fixed travel path through the world.
The player should gradually develop a mental map of the world through:
travel
exploration
journal discoveries
NPC information
landmarks
Points of Interest
journeys
dungeons
story events
The world should reward curiosity without requiring the player to manually control movement through every individual metre of terrain.
The main story should provide a clear forward direction while allowing the player to revisit prior locations and complete optional content.
Locations remain timeless once authored and available. Story developments do not transform previously visited locations. Instead, later story developments are represented through new locations, routes, POIs and destinations that become available along the player’s path.
This allows the player to progress at their own pace without requiring previously visited locations to change state.
A world map and regional maps are provided as imaginative starting points for understanding the setting, geography and relationships between locations. They are reference materials rather than interactive gameplay systems.
 
⸻
 
2. WORLD STRUCTURE
The world is organised hierarchically:
WORLD
 ├── Regions
 │    ├── Settlements
 │    ├── Wilderness
 │    ├── Routes
 │    ├── Points of Interest
 │    └── Dungeons
 │
 ├── Character Travel Paths
 │    ├── Forest Protagonist Path
 │    ├── Coastal Protagonist Path
 │    └── Mountain Protagonist Path
 │
 └── Global Story Developments
Regions form the primary geographic divisions.
Settlements provide safe civilisation.
Wilderness provides dangerous exploration.
Routes connect locations.
Character travel paths define the linear sequence of locations used by each protagonist’s main story.
POIs provide optional and narrative-rich discoveries along or near those paths.
Dungeons provide specialised high-risk content.
Global story developments determine which new locations, routes and destinations become available along the player’s path. They do not alter the state of previously authored locations.
The provided world map and regional maps establish the imaginative geography of the world but are not interactive map interfaces.
 
⸻
 
3. THE ESTABLISHED WORLD
The world contains a broad range of environments and societies.
Established major environments include:
temperate kingdom regions
ancient forests
high mountains
dwarven territories and cities
deserts
oases
coastal regions
marshes and swamps
ancient ruins
haunted battlefields
monster-infested wilderness
These are part of the same connected world.
The geography shown in the provided world and regional maps should guide the placement and relationships of these environments.
 
⸻
 
4. ELEVEN SIGNIFICANT SETTLEMENTS
The world contains:
Eleven significant settlements.
They are not interchangeable hubs.
Each settlement has its own:
geography
economy
population
local problems
nearby wilderness
political relationships
available services
story relevance
The settlements should form an interconnected economic and geographic network.
Not every settlement must be part of every protagonist’s main travel path.
Some settlements may be:
main-path locations
shared locations
optional destinations
regional hubs
locations reached through side content
Settlements remain timeless once authored and available. Later story developments should lead the player to new locations or destinations rather than changing the identity or state of an existing settlement.
 
⸻
 
5. THREE STARTING SETTLEMENTS
Three settlements serve as the starting points for the three protagonist storylines.
Forest Starting Settlement
A small farming settlement situated within or near an ancient forest.
The protagonist begins as a young person from this community.
They eventually join the King’s Army.
 
⸻
 
Coastal Starting Settlement
A coastal trading town.
The protagonist begins as an orphan/street rat.
After saving a merchant from bandits, they enter the merchant’s household.
 
⸻
 
Mountain Starting Settlement
A human settlement in the mountains.
The protagonist is a blacksmith apprentice.
They regularly travel to a nearby dwarven city to deliver tools and return with ore.
 
⸻
 
6. THREE STARTING AREAS
The starting areas provide deliberately different introductions to the same world.
Forest
Introduces:
farming
forests
wilderness
monsters
the kingdom
military life
Coast
Introduces:
trade
merchants
crime
bandits
maritime activity
piracy/smuggling
Mountains
Introduces:
blacksmithing
mining
dwarves
mountain travel
ore trade
deep-mountain threats
 
⸻
 
7. SHARED WORLD
The three starting areas are not separate campaigns.
They are geographically and narratively connected.
Events occurring in one region can affect the others.
For example:
Mountain monster activity
        ↓
Dwarven mining disrupted
        ↓
Ore shipments decrease
        ↓
Trade becomes more expensive
        ↓
Coastal merchants change routes
        ↓
Piracy/smuggling increases
        ↓
Kingdom experiences shortages
The player may encounter only parts of this chain initially.
The three protagonists may travel along different fixed paths while still passing through a shared world affected by common story developments.
Story developments should be expressed through new locations, routes, POIs and destinations becoming available along the player’s path.
The provided world map and regional maps may illustrate these relationships, but the player does not interact with them as navigable map systems.
 
⸻
 
8. REGION AND PATH UNLOCKING
The world should expand progressively.
The player initially has access to a limited section of the protagonist’s assigned travel path.
Story progression unlocks the next main-story locations along that path.
Unlock conditions may include:
story progression
completing a main-story objective
defeating a boss
meeting a requirement
establishing access to a route
resolving a regional event
The main story does not allow unrestricted travel to arbitrary distant regions.
A location becomes available when it is reached by the protagonist’s fixed path or unlocked through an explicitly authored side-content connection.
Story developments should unlock new locations rather than modify previously visited locations.
 
⸻
 
9. LINEAR MAIN STORY PATHS
Each protagonist has a fixed main-story travel path through the world.
Conceptually:
Starting Settlement
        ↓
Main-Path Location 1
        ↓
Main-Path Location 2
        ↓
Main-Path Location 3
        ↓
Major Story Destination
        ↓
Final Story Region
The exact locations and sequence are content decisions.
The path determines:
the order of main-story locations
the order of major story events
the intended progression of regional difficulty
the protagonist’s geographic movement
the locations available for main-story advancement
The path is linear for main-story purposes.
Later story developments are represented by new locations appearing farther along the path. Previously visited locations remain unchanged.
 
⸻
 
10. FORWARD AND BACKWARD TRAVEL
The player can travel along the protagonist’s established path in either direction.
Once a location has been reached, the player may return to prior locations.
Travel is therefore:
Forward along the path
        or
Backward along the path
The player cannot freely jump between unrelated locations outside the protagonist’s path unless a specific story or side-content connection allows it.
Returning to prior locations can be useful for:
services
quests
selling equipment
managing inventory
completing optional content
revisiting discovered POIs
repeatable journeys
reviewing information before continuing
Because locations are timeless, returning to them does not require the location to enter a new world state.
New story developments may instead make additional locations available farther along the path or reveal new POIs connected to the player’s current progression.
 
⸻
 
11. FREE EXPLORATION
The player has freedom within the protagonist’s current section of the world path.
This freedom includes:
choosing whether to investigate available POIs
choosing whether to enter available dungeons
choosing whether to complete side content
choosing whether to return to prior locations
choosing whether to travel forward or backward along the established path
choosing when to continue to newly available locations
The player does not freely select any destination in the entire world.
The main narrative remains linear, while exploration and optional content provide flexibility around that linear structure.
The player may postpone newly available optional content and continue at their own pace.
 
⸻
 
12. EXPLORATION MODEL
The game is not intended to simulate continuous physical walking.
Instead, exploration is represented through:
locations
fixed character paths
routes
journeys
encounters
POIs
events
discoveries
The player chooses an intended destination, path direction or activity.
The game resolves the associated travel and exploration gameplay.
The provided maps establish the imaginative geography and spatial relationships of the world. They do not function as interactive navigation screens.
 
⸻
 
13. LOCATIONS
A location is a meaningful geographic destination.
Examples include:
settlements
camps
ruins
mines
fortifications
landmarks
dungeons
wilderness destinations
other authored locations
Locations can contain multiple gameplay functions.
A location may be:
part of a protagonist’s main path
shared by multiple protagonists
optional
accessible through a POI
accessible through a dungeon
unlocked as a new destination along the path
Locations are timeless. Once available, a location retains its authored identity and does not transition through narrative world states.
 
⸻
 
14. SAFE AREAS
Safe areas are locations where the player is protected from ordinary wilderness threats.
Examples include:
settlements
established camps
other explicitly designated safe locations
Safe areas provide a place for the player to:
manage equipment
manage inventory
use consumables
interact with services
interact with NPCs
review story information
prepare for journeys
Safe areas may occur at both main-path locations and optional locations.
 
⸻
 
15. CONSUMABLE RESTRICTION
Consumables can only be used in safe areas.
This is an established gameplay rule.
Therefore:
Consumables cannot be activated during ordinary wilderness travel or combat.
This makes preparation before entering dangerous areas meaningful.
It also prevents consumables from becoming constant combat-management tools.
 
⸻
 
16. UNSAFE AREAS
Unsafe areas contain potential combat threats.
Entering or travelling through an unsafe area can result in:
enemy encounters
environmental events
story events
discovery events
POIs
Travel through unsafe areas is therefore a meaningful gameplay activity rather than simple menu navigation.
Unsafe areas may be located:
between main-path locations
around settlements
near POIs
inside dungeons
along optional side-content branches
 
⸻
 
17. COMBAT DURING TRAVEL
Unsafe-area travel requires combat where an encounter occurs.
The player does not manually move around enemies to avoid combat.
Instead, the Journey system resolves encounters as part of travel.
This integrates:
Exploration
+
Journey
+
Combat
+
Loot
+
Progression
 
⸻
 
18. JOURNEY ROUTES
Routes connect locations along a protagonist’s travel path.
A route can have:
direction
distance
danger rating
enemy tables
environmental events
POIs
story events
requirements
rewards
Routes may support:
forward travel
backward travel
one-way travel
story-gated access
The main-story route sequence remains fixed even when the player can travel backward along it.
Story developments may unlock new route segments farther along the path. Existing route segments remain unchanged unless their authored rules explicitly define otherwise.
 
⸻
 
19. ROUTE SAFETY
A route can be:
safe
relatively safe
dangerous
extremely dangerous
Route safety is authored as part of the route and does not need to change over time.
A later story development should generally introduce a new route or destination rather than transform an existing route.
Route danger may differ by direction or by the authored route definition.
 
⸻
 
20. STORY DEVELOPMENTS AFFECT EXPLORATION
Major narrative developments expand the player’s available path.
They may introduce:
new locations
new route segments
new POIs
new dungeons
new story destinations
new optional branches
new journal information
new NPC information
They do not transform previously visited locations into new states.
For example:
Mountain Story Development
        ↓
New Mountain Route Opens
        ↓
New Mining Settlement Becomes Available
        ↓
New Dwarven Dungeon Is Discovered
        ↓
New Trade Route Continues Toward the Kingdom
The world therefore responds to story progression through expansion along the player’s path rather than through changing the state of existing locations.
The provided maps may visually suggest these relationships, but the game presents them through authored destinations, travel choices and narrative information rather than an interactive map.
 
⸻
 
21. POINTS OF INTEREST
Points of Interest are optional or semi-optional exploration destinations.
POIs are primarily placed along, near or adjacent to a protagonist’s fixed travel path.
A POI may represent:
ruins
abandoned buildings
landmarks
graves
battlefields
unusual natural features
camps
caves
hidden paths
historical sites
mysterious objects
POIs provide side content without changing the linear order of the main story.
 
⸻
 
22. POI RELATIONSHIP TO MAIN PATHS
A POI is associated with one or more points along a protagonist’s travel path.
Conceptually:
Main Path Location A
        ↓
Route Segment
        ├── POI 1
        ├── POI 2
        └── Optional Dungeon
        ↓
Main Path Location B
The player may investigate the POI before continuing forward or return to it later.
POIs do not normally create unrestricted access to unrelated regions.
A later story development may reveal additional POIs along a newly available section of the path.
Previously discovered POIs remain available and unchanged.
 
⸻
 
23. POI DISCOVERY
POIs can be discovered through:
exploration along the current path
NPC information
quests
journal entries
previous discoveries
route travel
story progression
Not every POI needs to be explicitly marked on a map.
The provided maps are imaginative reference materials and do not need to display the player’s discovery state.
A POI may be:
visible from a route
revealed by an NPC
discovered through a side quest
unlocked by a journal clue
revealed when a new path segment becomes available
POI discovery does not require the player to complete the POI immediately.
 
⸻
 
24. POI CONTENT
A POI may provide:
a journal story bite
an item
an encounter
an NPC
a quest
lore
a combat encounter
a hidden path
a Story Relic
evidence about a larger world event
POIs are therefore a major side-content and narrative delivery mechanism.
POI content should enrich the main path without replacing the main story’s fixed progression.
POI content remains authored and timeless once discovered.
 
⸻
 
25. DISCOVERY STATE
Locations and POIs have discovery state.
Conceptually:
UNKNOWN
   ↓
DISCOVERED
   ↓
VISITED
   ↓
COMPLETED
Not every location needs all states.
For example, a settlement may remain simply discovered/visited, while a dungeon can have a completion state.
A POI may be discovered before the player chooses to visit it.
Discovery state records the player’s relationship with the location. It does not represent a changing world state.
 
⸻
 
26. DISCOVERY DOES NOT REQUIRE COMPLETION
Finding a location does not necessarily mean the player has completed its content.
For example:
Discover Dungeon
      ↓
Dungeon Available
      ↓
Later Journey
      ↓
Dungeon Completed
This distinction allows exploration and progression to occur at different times.
The player may continue along the main path and return later to complete optional content.
 
⸻
 
27. MAPS AS IMAGINATIVE REFERENCE MATERIAL
A world map and regional maps are provided as imaginative starting points for the setting.
They communicate:
broad geography
regional relationships
environmental placement
settlement context
approximate travel relationships
the overall shape and character of the world
They are not interactive gameplay systems.
The player does not use an interactive world map or regional map to:
select destinations
navigate between locations
inspect discovery state
reveal fog of war
choose arbitrary routes
track current position
access map-based travel controls
Travel is instead presented through authored location choices, path progression, journey interfaces, narrative descriptions and available destinations.
The maps may be included in design documentation, onboarding material or other non-interactive presentation contexts, but they do not define a required in-game map interface.
 
⸻
 
28. TRAVEL PRESENTATION WITHOUT AN INTERACTIVE MAP
The game should communicate travel through:
current location
available forward destination
available backward destination
route descriptions
journey descriptions
regional context
landmarks
NPC directions
journal information
narrative transitions
location and destination lists
A travel interface may present the player with authored choices such as:
Current Location
    ├── Continue Forward → Next Main-Path Location
    ├── Return Backward → Prior Visited Location
    └── Investigate → Available POI or Dungeon
This interface is a travel and destination-selection system, not an interactive map.
The player should understand where they are going through names, descriptions, narrative context and available choices rather than through direct map manipulation.
 
⸻
 
29. LOCATION INFORMATION
Locations and destinations may communicate:
location name
location type
safe/unsafe status
discovered status
current path position
forward/backward travel availability
available journey
recommended level
known route
dungeon status
relevant story marker
optional-content marker
newly unlocked destination marker
This information should be presented through location, journey, journal and travel interfaces rather than through an interactive map.
 
⸻
 
30. LOCATION SERVICES
Settlements can provide services.
Possible services include:
merchants
equipment management
inventory management
quest/story interactions
crafting
blacksmithing
guild services
other progression systems
Services are determined by settlement identity.
Not every settlement should contain every service.
Services are stable properties of the settlement unless a specific authored rule defines otherwise.
Story developments should generally introduce new settlements or new destinations with different services rather than changing the services of an existing settlement.
 
⸻
 
31. SETTLEMENT SPECIALISATION
Settlements should have meaningful differences.
For example:
Coastal Trading Town
    → Merchant / trade emphasis

Mountain Human Settlement
    → Blacksmith / mining relationship

Dwarven City
    → Metalwork / mining / specialised equipment

Military Settlement
    → Army / combat / supplies
These are examples of the intended design direction, not a requirement that every listed service be implemented exactly this way.
 
⸻
 
32. DWARVEN CITIES
Dwarven settlements are integrated into the world economy.
The mountain protagonist has an established relationship with a nearby dwarven city through:
delivering tools
returning with ore
This provides a natural early-game connection between:
blacksmithing
mining
dwarven society
trade
mountain travel
The dwarven city may be a main-path location for the mountain protagonist and an optional or shared location for other protagonists if their paths later connect to it.
Later mountain story developments should lead to new locations, mines, routes or dungeons connected to the dwarven city rather than changing the city’s state.
 
⸻
 
33. MOUNTAIN THREAT
The deep mountains are becoming increasingly dangerous.
Monsters emerging from deep mountain regions affect:
mining
dwarven cities
mountain settlements
travel routes
trade
This threat should be visible through both gameplay and narrative discoveries.
It should be represented through new dangerous locations, routes, dungeons and encounters along the mountain protagonist’s path.
The mountain protagonist’s fixed path may progress into these new locations without requiring previously visited mountain settlements to change state.
 
⸻
 
34. DESERT REGIONS
Desert regions contain:
settlements
oases
tribal populations
dangerous wilderness
trade routes
The desert tribes have unusually united and are conducting raids against distant settlements.
This creates a geopolitical threat that can affect exploration and travel outside the desert itself.
A desert region may appear on a protagonist’s path only when the main story reaches it or when an authored side-content connection makes it available.
New desert locations and routes should communicate the progression of this threat without requiring existing settlements to change state.
 
⸻
 
35. COASTAL REGIONS
Coastal areas contain:
trading settlements
maritime routes
merchants
pirates
smugglers
coastal wilderness
The coastal economy is particularly sensitive to disruption elsewhere in the world.
The coastal protagonist’s fixed path should use these regions to develop the themes of trade, crime, piracy and smuggling.
Later coastal story developments should introduce new ports, routes, ships, hideouts and coastal destinations rather than changing previously visited coastal settlements.
 
⸻
 
36. FOREST REGIONS
Ancient forest regions contain:
farming communities
wilderness
monsters
ruins
hidden locations
routes
story discoveries
The forest is one of the player’s earliest examples of the distinction between civilisation and dangerous wilderness.
The forest protagonist’s fixed path should begin in this environment before progressing toward the wider kingdom and military storyline.
New forest locations and POIs may become available as the protagonist advances, while previously visited forest locations remain timeless.
 
⸻
 
37. MARSHES AND SWAMPS
Marsh and swamp regions provide a distinct exploration environment.
They can contain:
isolated settlements
dangerous creatures
ruins
hidden locations
unusual routes
story discoveries
They should feel materially different from forests and mountains.
A marsh or swamp may serve as a main-path region, an optional side-content region or a shared region depending on the authored travel paths.
New marsh and swamp destinations should be added along the player’s path as appropriate rather than represented through changing states of existing locations.
 
⸻
 
38. ANCIENT RUINS
Ancient ruins are remnants of the lost human civilisation.
Most are extremely worn.
They may contain:
fragments of ancient mechanisms
inscriptions
artefacts
journal discoveries
monsters
clues to the ancient civilisation
evidence relating to the portal event
Ancient ruins are generally accessed as POIs, dungeons or authored locations along a protagonist’s path.
Each ruin is a timeless authored location. Additional discoveries should lead to other ruins or connected locations rather than changing the ruin itself.
 
⸻
 
39. HAUNTED BATTLEFIELDS
Haunted battlefields are locations where historical conflict has left supernatural remnants.
They can provide:
historical story bites
undead encounters
lost equipment
ghosts
clues about previous wars
connections to the wider history of the world
They are typically optional side-content locations discovered along or near a protagonist’s path.
Each battlefield remains a stable authored location once discovered.
 
⸻
 
40. WORLD HISTORY AS EXPLORATION
The player learns history through physical locations.
The world should answer questions such as:
“What happened here?”
rather than simply telling the player through an exposition screen.
Ruins, battlefields, settlements and artefacts are therefore narrative objects.
Because these locations are often optional, the player can discover the world’s history at their own pace while still following the main story’s fixed path.
Later historical revelations should be delivered through new locations, POIs, journal entries and destinations rather than by changing the meaning or state of previously visited locations.
 
⸻
 
41. EXPLORATION AND THE JOURNAL
Exploration directly feeds the Journal.
A discovery may trigger:
LOCATION DISCOVERED
        ↓
Story Bite Unlocked
        ↓
Journal Updated
        ↓
Related Story Information Available
The player can therefore reconstruct the world through exploration.
Journal discoveries may also:
reveal additional POIs
clarify the significance of a location
provide context for a main-story event
unlock optional quests
connect discoveries across different protagonist paths
point toward newly available locations
 
⸻
 
42. EXPLORATION AND QUESTS
Quests may direct the player toward a broad objective.
The game should favour:
“Investigate what is happening in the mountains.”
over:
“Travel exactly 3.2 km north and click the highlighted object.”
The player should retain a sense of discovery.
However, the main story should still communicate the next intended destination along the protagonist’s fixed path.
Side quests may direct the player to POIs near the current path or to previously visited locations.
Story developments should direct the player toward new locations rather than requiring existing locations to change state.
 
⸻
 
43. EXPLORATION AND NPC INFORMATION
NPCs can provide:
rumours
directions
warnings
historical information
references to locations
information about current events
NPC information can reveal locations or make existing discoveries more meaningful.
NPCs should generally point the player toward content connected to the protagonist’s current or previously visited path.
NPCs may also provide information about newly available destinations farther along the path.
 
⸻
 
44. DISCOVERY CHAINS
A single discovery can lead to another.
For example:
NPC Rumour
    ↓
Unknown POI
    ↓
Ruined Battlefield
    ↓
Journal Story Bite
    ↓
Historical Clue
    ↓
New POI Along the Path
    ↓
Ancient Ruin
This creates exploration-driven narrative progression without requiring unrestricted world travel.
Discovery chains should lead to new authored locations, POIs or route segments rather than changing the state of existing locations.
 
⸻
 
45. TRAVEL RISK
Travel through dangerous areas exposes the player to risk.
Risk can include:
combat
HP/resource expenditure
equipment loss under the established defeat rules
gold loss
time
opportunity cost
The player therefore has meaningful decisions about:
whether to investigate a POI now
whether to continue forward
whether to return backward
whether to enter a dungeon
whether to postpone optional content
whether to proceed to a newly unlocked location
The player does not normally choose between unrelated geographic routes outside the protagonist’s fixed path.
 
⸻
 
46. FORWARD TRAVEL VS RETURN TRAVEL
Forward travel advances the protagonist’s main-story position.
Return travel moves the protagonist toward previously visited locations.
Conceptually:
Current Location
    ├── Forward → Next Main-Path Location
    └── Backward → Prior Visited Location
Forward travel may unlock:
new story chapters
new regions
new settlements
new POIs
new dungeons
new story destinations
Backward travel may enable:
unfinished side content
newly revealed POIs
services
repeatable journeys
story follow-up information
revisiting previously discovered locations
Backward travel does not cause prior locations to change state.
 
⸻
 
47. SAFE ROUTES VS DANGEROUS ROUTES
Where geography permits, multiple route conditions can exist along the fixed path.
For example:
Forward Route
Dangerous
Main-story progression
New content

Backward Route
Previously travelled
Known route
Return access
A route’s safety is authored as part of the route.
Rather than changing an existing route from safe to dangerous, a later story development should generally introduce a new route segment, branch or destination with its own danger level.
Exact route configurations are content decisions.
The system supports linear path progression with reversible travel.
 
⸻
 
48. EXPLORATION REWARDS
Exploration can reward:
XP
gold
equipment
consumables
Story Relics
journal discoveries
quests
new POIs
new dungeons
narrative information
access to new locations
Exploration should not be reduced to a loot dispenser.
Information itself is a reward.
Optional content should provide meaningful rewards without making completion mandatory for main-story progression unless explicitly designed as a required story gate.
 
⸻
 
49. RETURN TRAVEL
Previously discovered locations remain known.
The player can return to them by travelling backward along the protagonist’s established path.
Returning can be useful for:
services
quests
selling equipment
managing inventory
completing optional content
repeatable journeys
reviewing journal information
following up on discoveries
The player cannot normally return to locations that have never been reached or discovered through the protagonist’s path.
Returning to a location does not trigger a world-state transition. The location remains as authored.
 
⸻
 
50. TIMELESS LOCATIONS AND STORY PROGRESSION
Locations remain timeless so that the player can progress at their own pace.
A location’s identity, services, geography and authored content do not change because the player advances the main story.
Story developments are represented through:
new locations
new route segments
new POIs
new dungeons
new story destinations
new journal entries
new NPC information
new optional branches
Conceptually:
Existing Location
        ↓
Player Progresses
        ↓
New Path Segment Available
        ↓
New Location Discovered
        ↓
New Story Development Encountered
This structure prevents the player from missing content because they did not visit a location at the intended narrative moment.
It also allows the player to complete optional content before or after advancing the main story.
 
⸻
 
51. NO FULL WORLD RESET
World discoveries and major story progress are persistent.
Ordinary defeat does not:
undiscover locations
reset routes
reset POIs
reset story events
remove newly unlocked locations
reset completed discoveries
move the player backward along the main story path
alter the timeless state of locations
 
⸻
 
52. EXPLORATION AND DEFEAT
If the player is defeated during dangerous travel:
the journey ends according to Journey rules
20% of current gold is lost
eligible spare items may be lost
equipped items remain protected
Epic, Legendary and Story items remain protected
XP is retained
story progress is retained
discovered locations remain discovered
completed POIs remain completed
unlocked path segments remain unlocked
the player’s main-story path position is retained
Exploration therefore carries risk without destroying long-term progress.
 
⸻
 
53. INVENTORY INTERACTION
Exploration can produce equipment and other loot.
The player must manage their inventory in accordance with the established inventory rules.
Consumables can only be used in safe areas.
Equipment can be managed according to the established equipment/inventory system.
 
⸻
 
54. WORLD SCALE
The world should feel large enough to support:
multiple storylines
multiple regions
long-term exploration
different environments
meaningful travel
distinct protagonist paths
shared world events
progressive discovery of new locations
but should not become so large that the player spends most of their time performing meaningless navigation.
The fixed-path structure allows the world to feel large while keeping the main story readable and purposeful.
The abstraction of travel allows the player to revisit prior locations without requiring excessive manual movement.
The timeless-location structure allows the player to progress at their own pace without requiring strict timing for optional exploration.
The provided maps establish the imaginative scale and geography of the world without requiring an interactive map system.
 
⸻
 
55. STORYLINE CONVERGENCE
The three starting perspectives progressively become connected.
Each protagonist begins with a local understanding:
Starting Settlement
      ↓
Starting Region
      ↓
Fixed Character Path
Then encounters:
Shared Events
      ↓
Connected Regions
      ↓
New Locations Along the Path
      ↓
Wider World
Eventually:
Three Perspectives
        ↓
Shared World Crisis
        ↓
Common Narrative
The protagonists may follow different geographic paths before reaching shared locations or experiencing the consequences of the same world events.
The world remains one coherent place throughout.
The provided maps may illustrate the geographic relationships between these paths, but the player experiences convergence through story, travel choices, locations and discoveries rather than through map interaction.
 
⸻
 
56. EXPLORATION DESIGN PRINCIPLE
The player should feel:
“I chose to investigate this place.”
rather than:
“The quest marker told me exactly where to click.”
The game should provide direction without eliminating curiosity.
The main story should clearly communicate the next forward destination, while POIs and side content should invite the player to look more closely at the path they are travelling.
Story progression should reveal new places to explore rather than requiring the player to revisit old places at a specific time.
The player should understand the world through authored travel choices, descriptions, landmarks, NPC information, journal entries and narrative context rather than through an interactive map.
 
